#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336014 :question/problem "![](images/1a/1acc2d6b8387947ff7186322796e273bcec366810ad73ac0025494ab627e06b2.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=1$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336015 :question/problem "![](images/be/be8468d84e3820bd1e9181d31aa3a1b7d1a85c743020a69cb67fc3f601a88363.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336019 :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=2t+1,\\,\\,\\,y=2t-1,\\,0\\le t\\le 5,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | ${\\color{blue}b}$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ |\n| $y$ | ${\\color{blue}a}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | ${\\color{blue}c}$ | $9$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a-b-c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $y=2t-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=2(0)-1=0-1=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=2t+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=2(2)+1=4+1=5.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=4$ into $y=2t-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=2(4)-1=8-1=7.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na-b-c=-1-5-7=-13.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338790 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+5 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5\\le 1+5 \\\\ 4 & \\le x\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $4\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 6 \\\\ -6-7 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7\\le 6-7 \\\\ -13 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-13\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $4\\le x\\le 6$ and $-13\\le y\\le -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338805 :question/problem "Differentiate $\\mathbf{f}(t)+4\\mathbf{g}(t)$ with respect to $t$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+4\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+4{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}4{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+4\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+4{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩+4⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩+⟨4,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}16{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+4,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}0+16{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1+8\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{t-4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}16{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338806 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}$ where $\\mathbf{f}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{ln}t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ t \\\\ {t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ t+2t\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ {t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338844 :question/problem "![](images/ba/baa279bad56527ad959b2923f25df848396ffc7060c612469d637d4e614117b5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(1,2)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338845 :question/problem "![](images/88/887895a47cf6ce3a6f109f754ef0c46eecaa6d68763baf3a696008e014d231af.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-2,-3)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=8.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-2))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={8}^{2} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338846 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=9?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} & ={3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=2,$ $b=-2,$ and $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(2,-2)$ and its radius is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338851 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x+3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=5?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x-(-3))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=-3,$ $b=-3,$ and $r=\\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-3,-3)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338867 :question/problem "![](images/b6/b6a4e9a8f4a1c6dcaaad453b843896f3fc56ae22a37d481dcb4cfe54cc53361d.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(7,3)$ and $(-1,3)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is  \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(7,3)$ and $Y(-1,3),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{{(7-(-1))}^{2}+{(3-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{7+(-1)}{2},\\frac{3+3}{2})=(3,3)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=4$ and center $(a,b)=(3,3),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338868 :question/problem "![](images/a6/a6a21dbf4785a270a0ae03066df1f92df73974532f9a75aa8e470cef42ea5e14.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-3,2)$ and $(3,4)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is  \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-3,2)$ and $Y(3,4),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{{(-3-3)}^{2}+{(2-4)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{2}=\\sqrt{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{-3+3}{2},\\frac{2+4}{2})=(0,3)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{10}$ and center $(a,b)=(0,3),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{10})}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338894 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,WXY$, if $W(2,-3,1)$, $X(-2,1,2)$, $Y(3,-1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX} & =\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}-12\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨6,9,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{9}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{261}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WXY$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{261}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338895 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,NPQ$, if $N(1,0,1)$, $P(2,2,3)$, $Q(2,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{NP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{NQ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{NP} & =\\mathbf{p}-\\mathbf{n} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{NQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{n} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{NP}\\times \\overset{\\to }{NQ} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{NP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{NQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{NP}\\times \\overset{\\to }{NQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,NPQ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338896 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,FGH$, if $F(1,1,2)$, $G(2,2,3)$, $H(1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{FH}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG} & =\\mathbf{g}-\\mathbf{f} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{FH} & =\\mathbf{h}-\\mathbf{f} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{FH} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{FG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{FH}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,FGH$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338898 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,CDE$, if $C(1,-2,3)$, $D(1,-3,3)$, $E(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{c} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{c} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(0\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+(0\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,CDE$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338899 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $MH$ of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(3,3,2)$, $M(1,1,2)$, $N(2,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338900 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $VH$ of the $△\\,TUV$, if $T(3,2,3)$, $U(0,1,0)$, $V(2,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $VH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =\\mathbf{u}-\\mathbf{t} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{t} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338901 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $ZH$ of the $△\\,WYZ$, if $W(2,3,2)$, $Y(1,2,1)$, $Z(1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $ZH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,WYZ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY\n$$\n\nSo, to find $ZH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =\\mathbf{z}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot (-4)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WYZ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $WY$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}WY & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ ZH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ZH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338902 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $GH$ of the $△\\,GSM$, if $S(1,-1,2)$, $G(-2,0,2)$, $M(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $GH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GSM$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM\n$$\n\nSo, to find $GH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG} & =\\mathbf{g}-\\mathbf{s} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{s} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}-6\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-6,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{76} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GSM$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{19}}{2}=\\sqrt{19}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $SM$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}SM & =|\\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =GH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338904 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $DH$ of the $△\\,DMA$, if $D(-1,2,1)$, $M(-1,0,-1)$, $A(1,-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $DH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,DMA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA\n$$\n\nSo, to find $DH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{m} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{m} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(0\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨8,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{96} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DMA$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{2}=2\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $MA$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MA & =|\\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =DH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ DH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ DH & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338911 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $R(3,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=20-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $R$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,2) & =20-(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $R(3,2)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 20-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $R(3,2)$ is the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=17.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338912 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $1$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3{x}^{2}y-6x{y}^{2}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=1$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =3(2)^{2}(1)-6(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =12-12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338913 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-1 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=2y-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338915 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $V(2,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{8y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $V$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2) & =\\frac{8(2)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $V(2,2)$ is the $2$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{8y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2 \\\\ 4y & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y+4 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $V(2,2)$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338917 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0,1,2)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)} & =(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}(x-0) \\\\ z-2 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338918 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,-6,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{x(y+x)}}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-6).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-6) & =\\frac{-6-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-6+(-2))}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,-6) & ={f}_{y}(-2,-6)(y-(-6)) \\\\ z-1 & =-1(y+6) \\\\ z-1 & =-y-6 \\\\ z & =-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,-6,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=-y-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-5$ (or $y+z+5=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-1,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338919 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,1,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3{e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|xy|$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,1) & =3{e}^{-1+1}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(-1)\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,1) & ={f}_{x}(-1,1)(x-(-1)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =3(x+1) \\\\ z+1 & =3x+3 \\\\ z & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,1,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3x+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3x+2$ (or $3x-z+2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-3\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-3⟩=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338920 :question/problem "Find ${(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}$ where $\\mathbf{f}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4t-1 \\\\ 3t+2 \\\\ 5t+6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=2t-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+6)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-3)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}4t-1 \\\\ 3t+2 \\\\ 5t+6\\end{bmatrix}+(2t-3)\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8t-2 \\\\ 6t+4 \\\\ 10t+12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8t-12 \\\\ 6t-9 \\\\ 10t-15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16t-14 \\\\ 12t-5 \\\\ 20t-3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338921 :question/problem "Find ${(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}$ where $\\mathbf{f}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 8 \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)={t}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =2t\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 8 \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+{t}^{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2t{e}^{t} \\\\ 16t \\\\ 2t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{t}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2t+{t}^{2}){e}^{t} \\\\ 16t \\\\ (2t-{t}^{2}){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338922 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{x(y-x)}{2x(y+1)}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,0) & =\\frac{(-1)(0-(-1))}{2(-1)(0+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,0) & ={f}_{y}(-1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $y-2z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,2,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338929 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, can be found by computing $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }i+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})‖} & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338930 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x+3y-2xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(1-2y)\\text{ }i+(3-2x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1) & =(1-2(1))\\text{ }i+(3-2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(\\frac{3}{2},1)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(\\frac{3}{2},1)‖} & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}}{1}=-\\mathbf{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338931 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}+x+y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,0) & =({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(0,0)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(0,0)‖} & =\\frac{2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338932 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =2(2)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨4,0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)0-(y-0)4 & =0 \\\\ -4y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338933 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2x+y-1=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-1} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ 1-x & =-y \\\\ x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338934 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}=4xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =4xy \\\\ {y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(6x-4y)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-3) & =(6(-1)-4(-3))\\,i+(2(-3)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-2\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,-3)=⟨6,-2⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{6} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-2} \\\\ (-2)(x+1) & =6(y+3) \\\\ -2x-2 & =6y+18 \\\\ 6y+2x+20 & =0 \\\\ 3y+x+10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338935 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4}{{y}^{2}}$, clear the denominator in $f(x,y)=c$ and classify the resulting conic equation in the entire $xy$-plane, including points excluded from the function's domain. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4}{{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4 & =c{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(3-c){y}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curves are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2},$ which consists of two vertical lines.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=4,$ which is an ellipse.\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2,$ which is a circle.\n\nSo, only statements “$c=0$ gives an ellipse” and “$c=1$ gives a circle” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338936 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)=2xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =2xy \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2y)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(0))\\,j \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,1)=⟨-1,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1} & =\\frac{y-1}{1} \\\\ -x & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338937 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=1,$ which is not a line.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=4,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=\\pm \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}},$ which consists of two lines.\n\nSo, only statements “$c=2$ gives a hyperbola” and “$c=0$ gives two lines” are true."}]}
